{
  "dom_id": "step-q124562",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Calculate \n${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{(n+3)(n+5)}.$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+5)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5}).$*",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n12{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+5)}.\n$$\n\nWe are also given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+3)(n+5)}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5}).\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n12{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5})=6{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+3}-\\frac{1}{n+5}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{0} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5})] \\\\ {s}_{1} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5})+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{2} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{3} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{7})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{4} & =6[(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{8})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{9})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that all of the terms cancel except $\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{4},$ and the $-\\frac{1}{N+4}$ and  $-\\frac{1}{N+5}$ terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=6[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{N+4}-\\frac{1}{N+5}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{(n+3)(n+5)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }6[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{N+4}-\\frac{1}{N+5}] \\\\  & =6{lim}_{N\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{N+4}-\\frac{1}{N+5}] \\\\  & =6[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-0-0] \\\\  & =6[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{7}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
